capability command/build  a command made from a program and its arguments, read by no shell
  heroes  process.command
  go      exec.Command, exec.Cmd, exec.Cmd.Path, exec.Cmd.Args
  rust    Command, Command::new, Command::get_program, Command::get_args, CommandArgs
  python  Popen, Popen.args
  note    a command is a record whose fields a program reads, which is what Rust's getters answer. Go's Args holds the program first; here `arguments` are those after it, as Rust's `args`

capability command/arguments  a command's arguments given or replaced
  heroes  process.with_arguments
  go      exec.Cmd.Args
  rust    Command::arg, Command::args
  python  -
  note    Rust adds one or several to those already there; here the list is given whole, since a program builds a `[str]` where it needs to add

capability command/shell  a text run through the system's shell
  heroes  process.shell
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  run(shell=True), Popen(shell=True), getoutput, getstatusoutput
  note    `/bin/sh -c` as Python's, and `cmd.exe /d /s /c` on Windows, where Python takes `ComSpec` too. Python's `getoutput(cmd)` is `capture(shell(cmd))` with the last line end taken off and the error joined; `getstatusoutput` adds the code, which `run` answers. On Windows a script holding `"` fails `unsupported`: the floor writes the child's line by `CommandLineToArgvW`'s rule, which `cmd.exe` does not undo (unrun on Windows)

capability command/quote  a text quoted so a POSIX shell reads it back as one word
  heroes  process.quote
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  shlex.quote
  note    the same answers as Python's on every input the tests ask: plain ASCII as it is, anything else in single quotes

capability command/quote-all  words quoted and joined into one line for a POSIX shell
  heroes  process.quote_all
  go      exec.Cmd.String
  rust    -
  python  shlex.join
  note    Python's. Go's `String` joins the words without quoting and is for showing only

capability command/windows-line  a child started from a Windows command line the program wrote, not from a list
  heroes  refused: the floor writes a child's Windows line from the list itself, by the rule `CommandLineToArgvW` undoes; a `Command` built from a line written by hand, Rust's `raw_arg`, is what lets a word run as another
  go      -
  rust    Command::raw_arg, Command::force_quotes
  python  -

capability text/quote-windows-command-line  a list of words written as the Windows command line that reads back as the same list
  heroes  strings.quote_windows_args
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  list2cmdline
  note    `strings`', a package below this one, for a program that writes a line for another to read; `process` needs none, since the floor writes a child's line from its list

capability env/set  a variable set for the child
  heroes  process.with_env
  go      exec.Cmd.Env, os.ProcAttr.Env
  rust    Command::env
  python  Popen(env=)
  note    over the inherited ones, as Rust's; Go's and Python's lists replace the whole environment, which is `with_env_cleared` then `with_env`. On Windows a name compares without its ASCII case, as Rust's does; a name holding a character above ASCII compares by its bytes there, where Windows would fold it too

