capability run/status  a command run to its end, and how it ended
  heroes  process.run
  go      exec.Cmd.Run
  rust    Command::status
  python  run, call, CompletedProcess, CompletedProcess.args, CompletedProcess.returncode
  note    a child that ends with another code than 0 is no failure of `run`, as Rust's and Python's; Go's `Run` answers it as an `ExitError`

capability run/captured  a command run to its end, what it wrote captured
  heroes  process.run
  go      exec.Cmd.Output, exec.ExitError.Stderr
  rust    Command::output, Output, Output.status, Output.stdout, Output.stderr, Child::wait_with_output
  python  run(capture_output=True), CompletedProcess.stdout, CompletedProcess.stderr, Popen.communicate
  note    captured through temporary files, so a child writing to both never fills a pipe and its time limit holds whatever it writes; Rust's `output` captures both where the command set neither, here what is `.pipe` is captured

capability run/text  a command's output as text, failing where it did not succeed
  heroes  process.capture
  go      exec.Cmd.Output
  rust    -
  python  check_output
  note    fails `failed` with the end of the error's text where that was captured, as Go's `ExitError.Stderr` carries it; `not_text` where the output is not UTF-8, where Python answers bytes unless asked for text

capability run/check  a child that did not succeed turned into a failure
  heroes  process.require_success
  go      exec.ExitError, exec.ExitError.Error
  rust    ExitStatus::exit_ok, Output::exit_ok, ExitStatusError, ExitStatusError::into_status
  python  check_call, run(check=True), CompletedProcess.check_returncode, CalledProcessError, SubprocessError
  note    `failed` for another code or a signal, `timeout` for the time limit

capability run/time-limit  a child ended once it has run too long
  heroes  process.with_time_limit
  go      exec.CommandContext, exec.Cmd.Cancel, exec.Cmd.WaitDelay, exec.ErrWaitDelay
  rust    -
  python  run(timeout=), Popen.wait(timeout=), Popen.communicate(timeout=), TimeoutExpired
  note    in nanoseconds on the monotonic clock, kept by `run`, `capture`, `wait` and `has_ended`; the child is ended at once and its status is `.timed_out`, where Python raises after killing it and Go's context kills it. Not a `time.Duration`: `process` uses no `time` (README § A1); a duration's `nanoseconds` is what a program passes. A read of a started child's pipe is not bounded by it

